    "Ages roughly 11–13. Subjects branch out into science, history, languages, and the arts." : {},
    "Ages roughly 14–17. A diploma is needed for most jobs and to apply to college or vocational programmes." : {},
    "Ages roughly 6–10. Reading, writing, basic math — the foundation everything else builds on." : {},
    "Agriculture" : {
      "comment" : "A field of work (job category): farming and growing food.\nField of study (degree subject): farming and food production."
    },
    "Agriculture & Food" : {
      "comment" : "An industry (sector of the economy): farming and food production."
    },
    "Aim for an elite university" : {
      "comment" : "Title of an advisor card: choose a top-ranked school for the degree"
    },
    "Aim for this" : {},
    "Airline Pilot Training (ATP Certificate)" : {},
    "Airline pilots need a special licence and lots of hours of flying. Most start by teaching flying or flying small planes, then join a bigger airline." : {
      "comment" : "Airline pilot note, point 1: the same fact in a beginner's or child's words"
    },
    "All the money you've earned so far." : {},
    "Almost everyone gets in. A great place to start if grades are tight." : {},
    "Ambition and initiative: imagining big goals, betting on yourself, and acting before there's a playbook." : {},
    "An airline pilot needs an airline transport licence, which takes years of building flying hours. Most pilots build hours as instructors or on small aircraft, then join a regional airline before a major one." : {
      "comment" : "Airline pilot note, point 1 (licence and flying hours): the real-world claim, for adults. General claim"
    },
    "An intensive full-stack software program. No licence — but the skills and portfolio it builds give you a real edge landing tech and engineering roles, and launching a software venture of your own." : {},
    "An open-source project you contribute to in the open. Land your work in something people depend on and your name travels with it." : {},
    "Analysts are hired in yearly classes and expected to work very long hours. Many leave after a couple of years for other industries; the rest face “up or out” promotions." : {
      "comment" : "Elite firm note, point 2 (long hours, up or out): the real-world claim, for adults. General claim"
    },
    "Announce an Investment Round" : {},
    "Announce the round" : {},
    "Any" : {
